The ZIP codes with the smallest pandemic-related declines in new business activity tended to have:

- more dense, diverse, and walkable built environments;
- lower levels of social vulnerability;
- lower percentages of young residents, age 18-39; and
- higher percentages of Black and Asian (non-Hispanic) residents.

The text also notes that observed COVID-19 case rates appeared to have a positive relationship with new business resilience after controlling for other factors, particularly for new chain and food service establishments, though this may reflect reverse causality because areas with relatively high levels of new food service business activity also had relatively higher proportions of food service employees, who were more at risk as “essential” workers.

The passage discusses higher education rather than high school. It states that ChatGPT, as a chatbot driven by GenAI, can be used to enhance education, provided its limitations are recognized.

Ways the passage identifies:

- For educators, ChatGPT can help in:
  - creating lesson plans for specific courses;
  - developing customized resources and learning activities, described as personalized learning support;
  - carrying out assessment and evaluation;
  - supporting the writing process of research.

- ChatGPT might also be used to enrich a reflective teaching practice by testing existing assessment methods to validate their scope, design, and capabilities beyond the possible use of GenAI, challenging academics to develop AI-proof assessments as a result and contributing to the authentic assessment of students’ learning achievements.

- More generally, the passage says ChatGPT can be used to:
  - generate answers to theory-based questions;
  - generate initial ideas for essays, while users examine the credibility of generated responses;
  - provide formative feedback on essays;
  - become a tutoring system by stimulating critical thinking and debates among students;
  - use language editing and translation skills to contribute towards increased equity in education by somewhat leveling the playing field for students from non-English-speaking backgrounds.

Concerns the passage identifies:

- One major concern is that ChatGPT can be used to reply to exam questions, write assignments, and draft academic essays without being easily detected by current versions of anti-plagiarism software.
- Evidence suggests that essays generated by ChatGPT can bypass conventional plagiarism detectors.
- ChatGPT can successfully pass graduate-level exams, which could potentially make some types of assessments obsolete.
- Accuracy and reliability issues include:
  - reliance on biased data, due to the limited scope of data used to train ChatGPT;
  - limited up-to-date knowledge, because training stopped in 2021;
  - generation of incorrect or fake information, such as fictitious references.
- Overreliance on ChatGPT could negatively impact students’ critical thinking and problem-solving skills.
- Responses from higher education institutions have been varied and fragmented, ranging from full bans to embracing it with student guidance; most guidance so far has been unclear or lacking detail about when use is allowed or considered acceptable, and most higher education institutions are reviewing their policies.
